  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1159 AM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nAcross the CONUS, mid-level troughing over the northeast CONUS \nwill be suppressed to the south as H5 ridging increases over the\nsouthwest and central CONUS. At the surface, high pressure from\nCanada is settling southward across the Great Lakes region, \nbringing a welcome break from the unsettled weather and extreme\nhumidity of the last several days. Northwest flow aloft will \nusher high-level wildfire smoke from the Pacific Northwest into \nthe area, but the smoke will stay well above the surface. \n\nUnsettled weather will return Friday evening into Saturday. An\nH5 trough will pivot around the northern periphery of the H5\nridge into the northern Rocky Mountains. Surface cyclogenesis\nwill commence on the lee side of the mountains, and an area of \nlow pressure will move from NW to SE into the region by Friday \nafternoon. This will initiate isolated to scattered \nthunderstorms during the afternoon and evening, but the best \nchance of precipitation (70-90%) will be late Friday night into \nSaturday morning ahead of the associated cold front. \n\nCurrent thinking is the risk for severe thunderstorms will be\nlow due to the overnight timing limiting instability. SPC does\nhave a marginal risk it its Day 3 outlook for a small part of\nthe Ozark Foothills. If the timing of this system speeds up or\nslows down significantly, the chances of a few severe\nthunderstorms would increase with the corresponding increase in\ndaytime heating and instability. Current ensemble guidance gives\nthe mostly likely spread of rainfall amounts to be generally\n0.25-0.75\" across the region. \n\nFollowing the frontal passage, a cut off low will become\ncentered over the Mid-Atlantic and southern Appalachians. This\nwill provide weak forcing to initiate isolated to widely\nscattered (20-40% coverage) diurnally-driven convection from\nSunday into the middle of next week. There will a lot of dry\nhours during this time as well, so it will not be a washout.\nTemperature and humidity levels will remain near normal values\nfor early August.",
